---
/**
 * How a douar gets its Latin name. Every sentence here has to stay true of api/src/lib/:
 * douarNames.ts, douarPlaces.ts, translit.ts, spell.ts and translitWords.ts, of
 * api/src/worker/suggest.ts, and of the scripts that run them. Its numbers come from
 * `naming`, read from the data and that code, and site/tests/naming.test.ts fails on any
 * other number typed here. The mirror of this page under /fr/ says the same in French.
 */
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "../../i18n/ui";
import { numbers, percent } from "../../lib/format";
import { naming, spell } from "../../lib/naming";

const locale = "en";

const toc = [
  { id: "found", label: "Found in a source" },
  { id: "chance", label: "Tested against chance" },
  { id: "spelt", label: "Spelt by the engine" },
  { id: "typed", label: "Typed either way" },
  { id: "fractions", label: "Fractions" },
  { id: "visitors", label: "From visitors" },
  { id: "sources", label: "Where it lives" },
];

const n = numbers(locale);
const count = (value: number) => n.format(value);
const share = (fraction: number) => percent(locale, fraction * 100, { digits: 0 });
const exactly = (fraction: number) => percent(locale, fraction * 100, { digits: 1 });

const file = (path: string) => `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${path}`;
---

<Docs
  locale={locale}
  route="docs/names/"
  title="How douars get their Latin names"
  description="Where each douar’s Latin name comes from: a source that writes it, matched to HCP’s Arabic and tested against chance, or an engine that spells it from the Arabic."
  lede={`HCP names all ${count(naming.douars)} douars in Arabic only. ${count(naming.sourced)} have a Latin name a source writes, matched to HCP’s Arabic and tested against chance. An engine built from Moroccan names spells the rest, offline.`}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="note"><a href="/douars/">Find a douar</a></p>

  <section id="found">
    <h2>Found in a source</h2>
    <p>
      The Ministry of Education’s lists of public schools give each school’s name and address in both scripts, and
      a rural school is named after the douar it serves. That names {count(naming.education)} douars.
      OpenStreetMap names {count(naming.osm)} more, from its places and from the schools, mosques and other
      features named after a douar, and GeoNames {count(naming.geonames)}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each name is matched to HCP’s Arabic among the douars of its own commune only, so 2 villages that share a name
      in different provinces never cross.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="chance">
    <h2>Tested against chance</h2>
    <p>
      Every matching pass runs a second time with the communes swapped, each for another of a like size, so whatever
      it still finds is chance: {exactly(naming.chance.schools)} of the names for the school lists, and{" "}
      {exactly(naming.chance.places)} for the maps. A second map pass takes a name near the douars of the same
      fraction already placed, and its check gives each douar the name of one in another commune instead:{" "}
      {exactly(naming.chance.near)}. A pass over {share(naming.chance.most)} writes nothing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="spelt">
    <h2>Spelt by the engine</h2>
    <p>
      The other {count(naming.spelt)} are spelt from the Arabic, and show in italics. The engine reads a name in
      either script down to its consonants, and the 2 meet for {exactly(naming.keysMeet)} of the{" "}
      {count(naming.hcpNames)} names HCP gives in both. On top of that sit {count(naming.words)} words learned from names
      written in both scripts, so <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">تالوين</span> comes out {spell("تالوين")}, as HCP writes
      the commune. Rules for the way French writes Moroccan names spell a word it hasn’t seen:{" "}
      <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">تيفنوين</span> is {spell("تيفنوين")}.
    </p>
    <p>
      On sourced douars held back from it, the engine spells {share(naming.rules.exact)} exactly as the source
      does, and {share(naming.rules.withinALetter)} within a letter. Where the Ministry and a map both name a douar,
      they spell it the same way {share(naming.agree.exact)} of the time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="typed">
    <h2>Typed either way</h2>
    <p>
      The same consonants work from Latin letters, so a douar typed as it sounds on <a href="/douars/">the douars
      page</a> is found by its Arabic name, however its vowels are written.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="fractions">
    <h2>Fractions</h2>
    <p>
      Where a fraction shares its name with one of its commune’s douars, it takes the Latin name a source gives that
      douar: {count(naming.fractionsNamed)} of the {count(naming.fractions)}. HCP lists {count(naming.outside)}{" "}
      as a fraction outside the commune and {count(naming.notional)} as a notional one, and those show the label
      translated. The engine spells the rest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="visitors">
    <h2>From visitors</h2>
    <p>
      A commune page takes a spelling for a douar no source names. It goes live once {count(naming.votes)}{" "}
      visitors suggest it, and only if its consonants meet the douar’s Arabic, so another name can’t be slipped in.
      What’s kept about a visitor is on <a href="/docs/privacy/">the privacy page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sources">
    <h2>Where it lives</h2>
    <p>
      The engine is in <a href={file("api/src/lib/translit.ts")}><code>translit.ts</code></a> and{" "}
      <a href={file("api/src/lib/spell.ts")}><code>spell.ts</code></a>, and <code>pnpm translit</code> spells a
      name either way or scores the engine. The matching is in{" "}
      <a href={file("api/src/lib/douarNames.ts")}><code>douarNames.ts</code></a> and{" "}
      <a href={file("api/src/lib/douarPlaces.ts")}><code>douarPlaces.ts</code></a>, and the names it found are in{" "}
      <a href={file("api/data/")}><code>api/data/</code></a> with their sources.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
